 ## Sync and claim
 Tracker state is shared the moment it changes, not when a PR merges, so parallel terminals see each other's work.
 - **Sync first.** Every skill that reads or changes tracker state starts with `./zn sync` (a merge of `origin/main` in a worktree, a fast-forward in the main checkout). Reviewers and stream agents never merge: they run `./zn sync --fetch`, which only updates `origin/main`, so the diff they judge does not move.
-- **Statuses** (`status:` in an item's frontmatter): `new -> preparing -> prepared -> active -> done`. `design:` (draft, reviewed, ready) stays the design review's field. A missing `status:` is derived: `done/` is done, `active/` is active, `design: ready` is prepared, a draft item in a raft (or a draft raft) is preparing, anything else is new. `./zn status` lists them from `origin/main`; `status <id>` prints one; `status <id> <to>` moves one, checked against `origin/main`. `done` is only ever set by `./zn done`, and only `ready` (or the legacy `start` of a backlog raft) claims `active`, and `stop` gives a readied raft back.
+- **Statuses** (`status:` in an item's frontmatter): `new -> preparing -> prepared -> active -> done`, and `blocked` (c0250) beside them: `preparing -> blocked` and `active -> blocked` are taken by `./zn block` only, and `blocked -> new` and `blocked -> prepared` by `./zn resolve` only (`./zn status <id> blocked` and `./zn status <id> <to>` from a blocked item are refused). A blocked item carries `blocked: <reason>` (one of `review-exhausted`, `design-gap`, `needs-decision`, `red-gate`, `quota`, `tripwire`) and holds no claim; its `rank:` is never changed. `quota` is a block only when every CLI stays out of quota past `quota_cap_hours` (`.models.toml`, `[dispatch]`, whole hours, at least 1), which c0253's worker waits for. `design:` (draft, reviewed, ready) stays the design review's field. A missing `status:` is derived: `done/` is done, `active/` is active, `design: ready` is prepared, a draft item in a raft (or a draft raft) is preparing, anything else is new. `./zn status` lists them from `origin/main`; `status <id>` prints one; `status <id> <to>` moves one, checked against `origin/main`. `done` is only ever set by `./zn done`, and only `ready` (or the legacy `start` of a backlog raft) claims `active`, and `stop` gives a readied raft back.
 - **Every write is stamped**: the stored value is `<state>-<three words>` (for example `preparing-quiet-copper-falcon`) and each write uses fresh words, so two terminals making the same move conflict in git instead of merging silently.
-- **Published or carried.** These moves are pushed straight to `origin/main` as a tiny commit: `new issue` (the stub, `--desc "<line>"` is its Problem), `new raft` (claims the raft, and its members when they are `new`), `fold` and `recombine` (claim their rafts), `release`, `status <id> preparing|new`, `status <id> prepared` back from `active`, and the claim made by `ready` (or by a legacy `start`). An emergent `new issue --raft <R-id>` and `renumber <id>` without `<new>` push a counter-only commit (the raised `z-nile/next`) and then work locally. The move to `prepared` is **carried by the prep PR**: it only edits the local documents.
-- **The direct-push exception.** Everything else lands by pull request. A published change is a tracker-only commit: only `<root>/(issues|rafts)/<ID>/<ID>.md` and `check.sh`, under a root that `origin/main`'s `z-nile/projects.toml` lists (never the working tree's), a `check.sh` only when it is created, an existing document only in its `status:` line (what `status`, `new raft` and `start` publish), and `z-nile/next`, whose counters may only go up. An issue's `spend.md` is not pushed by `./zn spend`: the command commits the appended row on the current branch, and the row reaches main with that branch. `publish.commit` still accepts an appended `spend.md` when a tracker commit carries one (a directory move keeps it byte for byte). The one other kind is a whole-directory move by `ready` or `stop`: every file of an item directory moves between the backlog and `active/` of its own root (never `done/`, never to another root), byte for byte, except the document's `status:` line and the item paths in `check.sh`, which `paths.rewrite` changes to the new directory; publish recomputes both and refuses anything else. `./zn land` also pushes a capture's edits to draft backlog issue documents (their sections, `title` and `depends_on`; design stays draft, every other frontmatter line unchanged) after the static stages (`z-nile/check.sh --static`). Designs, reviews, code and acceptance specs never ride it. A commit that skips the deploy for the many tiny pushes is intended: `deploy.yml` ignores every project root (`<root>/**`: `pickman/.tracker/**`, `z-nile/.tracker/**` and the rest, in registry order) and `z-nile/next` on `push`. Adding a project needs its registry entry and its root in that `deploy.yml` list. A project with code also needs its home (z-nile/README.md, A project's home), which the gate checks for every project.
+- **Published or carried.** These moves are pushed straight to `origin/main` as a tiny commit: `new issue` (the stub, `--desc "<line>"` is its Problem), `new raft` (claims the raft, writes `reviews: judged` into it, and claims its members when they are `new`), `fold` and `recombine` (claim their rafts), `release`, `status <id> preparing|new`, `status <id> prepared` back from `active`, and the claim made by `ready` (or by a legacy `start`). An emergent `new issue --raft <R-id>` and `renumber <id>` without `<new>` push a counter-only commit (the raised `z-nile/next`) and then work locally. The move to `prepared` is **carried by the prep PR**: it only edits the local documents.
